// Escaping text for HTML.
//
// One function, and it is for GENERATING HTML: a value a program is about to
// put into a page -- a file name, a commit message, a user's display name --
// goes through `escape` on its way in, and then it is text and not markup.
//
// **All five characters, always.** `&`, `<` and `>` are what element content
// needs; `"` and `'` are what an attribute value needs, and which quote the
// attribute was written with is not something this function can see. So there
// is no `quote` flag: the one spelling is the one that is safe in element
// content AND inside either kind of quoted attribute. (It is not safe in an
// unquoted attribute, in a `<script>` or `<style>` body, or in a URL attribute
// that could hold `javascript:` -- those are different contexts, and no
// escaping function makes them safe.)
//
// The output is CPython's `html.escape(s)`, byte for byte: `&amp;`, `&lt;`,
// `&gt;`, `&quot;` and `&#x27;`.
//
// **There is no `unescape`**, and that is the design rather than a gap.
// Reading HTML back means decoding every named character reference HTML5
// defines -- over two thousand of them, some without a trailing semicolon --
// and a decoder that knows only the five above is a codec with a hole in it:
// it turns `&eacute;` into itself and `&amp;eacute;` into `&eacute;`,
// silently. A program that has to parse HTML needs a parser.
//
// **`&` goes first**, so the `&` in every reference the later four insert is
// not escaped a second time. Every character replaced is ASCII, and UTF-8
// never uses an ASCII byte inside a multi-byte character, so the search cannot
// cut one in half.
//
// The five replacements used to be five splits and five joins, and a private
// `replace` of this module's own, because `str` has none. They are
// `text.replace` now: the same split and join, written once.
import text;

/// `content` made safe to use as HTML text or inside a quoted attribute value.
///
///     html.escape("<a href='x'>Tom & Jerry</a>")
///         == "&lt;a href=&#x27;x&#x27;&gt;Tom &amp; Jerry&lt;/a&gt;"
pub str escape(str content) {
    str out = text.replace(content, "&", "&amp;");
    out = text.replace(out, "<", "&lt;");
    out = text.replace(out, ">", "&gt;");
    out = text.replace(out, "\"", "&quot;");
    return text.replace(out, "'", "&#x27;");
}
